  "unitDescription": {
    "value": "In this 10-week unit, students apply computational and design thinking to address complex, open-ended robotics challenges. By decomposing real-world scenarios, students define precise problem specifications and user stories to guide the iterative development of autonomous solutions. Students design, implement, and refine algorithms using object-oriented programming to control robotic hardware, employing flowcharts and pseudocode to verify the accuracy of their logic and ensure robust solution performance. Throughout the development process, students utilise systematic testing and debugging techniques to validate their solutions against specific design criteria.\n\nFurthermore, students explore the interaction between hardware and software components, managing data flow and control structures within digital systems. By documenting their design process and evaluating the effectiveness of their robotic solutions, students reflect on the enterprising nature of their work and the broader impact of autonomous technologies. This unit fosters collaborative project management skills, requiring students to plan tasks, manage timelines, and evaluate their prototypes against stakeholder needs and functionality requirements.",
    "aiNotes": ""
  },
  "cohortAndClassConsiderations": {
    "value": "The Years 9–10 cohort consists of a diverse group of boys with a wide range of digital competencies, from students with strong technical skills and personal interest in IT, to those who require further support to confidently engage with digital systems. Many students come from regional or rural backgrounds, where access to structured digital learning opportunities may have been limited. The program is designed to build on prior knowledge while introducing more advanced and applied skills relevant to future study and employment. The curriculum supports the school’s priority to equip students with transferable digital skills, critical thinking abilities, and an understanding of ethical technology use in a rapidly changing world.",
    "aiNotes": ""
  },
